{"series":{"id":"cost-of-living::rpp-housing","label":"Regional price parity · Housing services / rents","dataset_id":"frozen-atlas","unit":"BEA regional price-level index","scope":"us_states","definition":"Housing expenditures consist of the monetary rents paid by tenants for tenant-occupied housing, an imputed rental value for owner-occupied dwellings (measured as the income the homeowner could have received if the house had been rented to a tenant), the rental value of farm dwellings, and spending on group housing. Housing price levels are only estimated for tenant-occupied housing. Published SARPP line 3; common BEA RPP scale. The 2024 published U.S. housing services / rents index is 100.629, retained as the comparison reference. No component has been rescaled to 100. Housing price levels are estimated from tenant-occupied rents; this is not a home-purchase-price index.","denominator":"BEA common national RPP base; all-items U.S.=100; component national values retain source scaling","reporting_period":"2024 annual price-level comparison; February 19, 2026 release","source_url":"https://apps.bea.gov/regional/zip/SARPP.zip","sources":[{"id":"bea-rpp-2024","title":"U.S. BEA · Regional price parities by state, SARPP","url":"https://apps.bea.gov/regional/zip/SARPP.zip","date":"February 19, 2026 release; 2024 data and revised 2008–2023","note":"Price-level comparisons across states for a given year on BEA’s common RPP scale. All-items U.S.=100; published U.S. component rows are not necessarily 100. They are preserved without renormalization, with actual component references attached. RPP is not a household living budget or a year-to-year inflation rate.","license":"U.S. federal government public information; source attribution retained."}],"limitations":["RPP compares state price levels within a given year. It is not a year-to-year inflation rate, a complete family living budget or a required-income estimate.","All-items U.S. RPP is 100, while published U.S. component rows are not all exactly 100. The original indexes and each actual national component reference are preserved without renormalization.","Housing-services RPP reflects rents, including how rental evidence supports the accounts; it is not a home-purchase-price index. ACS gross rent is monthly rent plus estimated tenant-paid utilities/fuels.","ACS B25071 directly publishes the median household rent-to-income percentage among relevant renter units. It is not calculated by dividing two unrelated state medians, and it is not the fraction of renters above a 30% burden threshold.","ACS rent and rent-burden MOEs define 90% confidence intervals. RPP rows do not include comparable sampling intervals here.","State price and rental summaries cannot identify an individual household’s affordability, needed spending or housing preferences."],"interval_type":null,"display_reference":100.629,"years":[2024],"places":[{"id":"AL","name":"Alabama"},{"id":"AK","name":"Alaska"},{"id":"AZ","name":"Arizona"},{"id":"AR","name":"Arkansas"},{"id":"CA","name":"California"},{"id":"CO","name":"Colorado"},{"id":"CT","name":"Connecticut"},{"id":"DE","name":"Delaware"},{"id":"DC","name":"District of Columbia"},{"id":"FL","name":"Florida"},{"id":"GA","name":"Georgia"},{"id":"HI","name":"Hawaii"},{"id":"ID","name":"Idaho"},{"id":"IL","name":"Illinois"},{"id":"IN","name":"Indiana"},{"id":"IA","name":"Iowa"},{"id":"KS","name":"Kansas"},{"id":"KY","name":"Kentucky"},{"id":"LA","name":"Louisiana"},{"id":"ME","name":"Maine"},{"id":"MD","name":"Maryland"},{"id":"MA","name":"Massachusetts"},{"id":"MI","name":"Michigan"},{"id":"MN","name":"Minnesota"},{"id":"MS","name":"Mississippi"},{"id":"MO","name":"Missouri"},{"id":"MT","name":"Montana"},{"id":"NE","name":"Nebraska"},{"id":"NV","name":"Nevada"},{"id":"NH","name":"New Hampshire"},{"id":"NJ","name":"New Jersey"},{"id":"NM","name":"New Mexico"},{"id":"NY","name":"New York"},{"id":"NC","name":"North Carolina"},{"id":"ND","name":"North Dakota"},{"id":"OH","nam